WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.680
So, when you turn on a faucet in your kitchen, you expect water to come out, right?

00:05.820 --> 00:09.440
The reason you expect that water to come out is basically because you believe in a pipeline.

00:10.220 --> 00:16.220
You know there is this solid, unbroken, traceable path of copper, or, I don't know, PVC,

00:16.700 --> 00:21.780
running from the reservoir, under the streets, through your walls, and right up to your sink.

00:21.900 --> 00:24.320
Yeah, exactly. A leads to B leads to C.

00:24.320 --> 00:29.040
Right. And if the water stops, well, you just walk backward along the pipe until you find the leak.

00:30.000 --> 00:34.600
It is this perfectly logical, highly comforting view of reality.

00:35.180 --> 00:40.880
We build pretty much our entire understanding of the world on that kind of traceable causality.

00:41.240 --> 00:44.200
But today, we are going completely off the pipeline.

00:44.300 --> 00:47.200
In fact, we are looking at research that fundamentally blows the pipeline up.

00:47.480 --> 00:48.680
It really does more than just blow it up.

00:48.680 --> 00:56.040
I mean, it argues that in the most complex, robust systems in the entire universe, a pipeline is actually a huge vulnerability.

00:56.520 --> 00:56.840
Wow.

00:56.840 --> 01:01.000
Yeah. The source material we're looking at today completely dismantles that, you know,

01:01.060 --> 01:04.080
that A to B to C expectation of reality that you just described.

01:04.380 --> 01:06.100
So let's give an overview for you guys listening.

01:06.620 --> 01:14.720
We are pulling from an incredibly dense, massive, cross-disciplinary 2026 paper by an independent researcher named Flixson.

01:15.100 --> 01:17.540
It's titled, Function Survives Collapse.

01:17.540 --> 01:26.180
And our mission for this deep dive is to explore this really mind-bending unified theory that somehow connects secure computer cryptography,

01:26.600 --> 01:34.800
the biology of bacterial cells, the development of plant embryos, and even the design of the fonts you're probably reading on a screen right now.

01:34.800 --> 01:39.280
Which, on the surface, that just sounds like a random assortment of entirely unrelated fields.

01:39.420 --> 01:39.560
Oh.

01:39.760 --> 01:42.240
I mean, a computer network is clearly not a plant seed.

01:42.660 --> 01:47.820
But Flixson argues they are all driven by the exact same underlying mathematical engine.

01:48.180 --> 01:51.320
They all share this fundamental architecture of survival.

01:51.560 --> 01:52.680
Okay, let's unpack this.

01:52.680 --> 01:59.340
The core radical idea here is that the most robust, successful systems, whether that's a privacy network or a living cell,

02:00.000 --> 02:04.380
they don't work by preserving every microscopic detail in a perfect pipeline.

02:04.660 --> 02:07.980
They work by deliberately destroying the path taken to reach a result.

02:07.980 --> 02:13.940
They force the process to collapse so that only the final functional output actually survives.

02:14.740 --> 02:17.060
And that is just a crucial shift in thinking.

02:17.360 --> 02:22.040
Because when humans design systems, we are generally obsessed with provenance.

02:22.040 --> 02:23.560
We want an audit trail.

02:23.880 --> 02:24.660
Right, we want the receipts.

02:24.960 --> 02:25.480
Exactly.

02:25.820 --> 02:30.880
We want to know exactly how a piece of data got from a server in Tokyo to a laptop in New York.

02:31.360 --> 02:36.260
And biologists historically wanted to track exactly how a specific piece of genetic code

02:36.260 --> 02:40.100
becomes a specific folded protein step by step.

02:40.640 --> 02:43.640
Flixson's paper basically argues that this pipeline ontology,

02:43.960 --> 02:47.520
this absolute obsession with the journey of the data, is fundamentally flawed.

02:47.520 --> 02:53.900
Or at the very least, it's the wrong way to look at how systems actually achieve extreme robustness.

02:54.220 --> 02:57.620
So to really get this, we need to understand the concept that replaces the pipeline.

02:58.240 --> 03:01.480
The paper introduces this mathematical idea called admissible projection.

03:01.980 --> 03:04.060
But before we get to the computer science or the bacteria,

03:04.520 --> 03:07.620
what exactly are we looking at when we talk about an admissible projection?

03:08.000 --> 03:12.160
Well, to visualize it, you really have to picture two entirely different layers of reality,

03:12.280 --> 03:13.180
like two different spaces.

03:13.180 --> 03:16.280
First, you have the space of microscopic configurations.

03:16.860 --> 03:19.880
In the mathematical notation of the paper, this is called X.

03:20.000 --> 03:20.940
Okay, space X.

03:21.020 --> 03:21.240
Right.

03:21.560 --> 03:25.820
And this is the raw, chaotic, high-dimensional reality of a system.

03:26.200 --> 03:30.760
It's every possible path, every fluctuating atom, every single line of code,

03:30.860 --> 03:32.660
every bouncing molecule in a fluid.

03:33.100 --> 03:38.100
It is vastly complex, highly turbulent, and practically infinite.

03:38.200 --> 03:41.360
And I'm assuming just completely impossible to fully track.

03:41.360 --> 03:44.900
Yeah, it is computationally and physically intractable to track it all.

03:45.180 --> 03:49.340
But then, layered above that, you have a second space, which Flixion calls M.

03:49.940 --> 03:52.020
And this is the admissible invariant manifold.

03:52.180 --> 03:52.340
Okay.

03:52.820 --> 03:55.000
Admissible invariant manifold is a lot of syllables.

03:55.880 --> 03:56.700
Translate that for me.

03:56.840 --> 03:57.100
Sure.

03:57.700 --> 04:01.380
Think of it as the realm of the stable functional output.

04:01.800 --> 04:02.980
It is the finished product.

04:03.440 --> 04:05.500
It's the final answer to a math problem,

04:05.500 --> 04:08.020
or the fully formed leaf of a plant,

04:08.540 --> 04:10.920
or just the successful transmission of a message.

04:11.360 --> 04:15.180
The admissible projection, which the paper represents with the Greek letter pi,

04:15.440 --> 04:17.760
is the mathematical map, or the process,

04:18.140 --> 04:21.480
that takes you from that chaotic, microscopic space, X,

04:21.660 --> 04:25.780
and lands you securely on that stable, functional space, M.

04:26.000 --> 04:27.460
Okay, I think I'm picturing it.

04:27.460 --> 04:31.880
But if it's just a map from X to M, how is that any different from a pipeline?

04:32.440 --> 04:35.460
I mean, a pipeline is literally a map from the reservoir to my sink.

04:35.880 --> 04:36.780
That is the perfect question,

04:36.860 --> 04:39.440
because an admissible projection is not a simple transfer.

04:39.760 --> 04:42.260
If it were just a simple transfer, well, it would just be a pipeline,

04:42.400 --> 04:43.200
and it would be fragile.

04:43.720 --> 04:47.360
Flixion lays out four very specific non-negotiable rules

04:47.360 --> 04:50.540
for this projection map that make it entirely different from a pipe.

04:50.620 --> 04:51.760
Let's walk through those rules then.

04:52.120 --> 04:53.860
Rule one, it preserves the function.

04:53.860 --> 04:57.580
So whatever the system is actually trying to compute, build, or achieve,

04:57.800 --> 05:00.140
that overarching result makes it across intact.

05:00.420 --> 05:04.360
If the system's job is to say yes or no, that yes or no survives.

05:04.620 --> 05:05.360
Well, that makes sense.

05:05.420 --> 05:07.800
If it didn't preserve the function, the system would just be broken.

05:08.220 --> 05:08.700
Precisely.

05:09.360 --> 05:12.360
Rule two is that it is utterly irreversible.

05:12.600 --> 05:15.400
You absolutely cannot run the equation backward.

05:16.020 --> 05:16.500
Wait, really?

05:16.700 --> 05:16.900
Yeah.

05:17.280 --> 05:19.660
Once you land on the stable manifold M,

05:19.980 --> 05:23.220
you cannot look at it and deduce the microscopic state of X.

05:23.220 --> 05:27.400
Hold on, but we do reverse engineering all the time in science and software.

05:28.040 --> 05:31.560
Are you saying it's just difficult or, like, mathematically impossible?

05:31.920 --> 05:34.480
I am saying it is mathematically and physically impossible.

05:34.740 --> 05:38.160
It's a one-way street by the sheer laws of thermodynamics,

05:38.560 --> 05:40.440
which actually brings us to rule three.

05:41.060 --> 05:43.180
The projection map does not decrease entropy.

05:43.540 --> 05:44.020
Oh, interesting.

05:44.340 --> 05:44.520
Right.

05:44.640 --> 05:47.640
In fact, the thermodynamic randomness of the system

05:47.640 --> 05:50.180
generally increases as the function is projected.

05:50.460 --> 05:53.040
The system gets messier at the microscopic level.

05:53.040 --> 05:56.980
And that leads to rule four, which is the real paradigm shifter.

05:57.620 --> 06:00.960
Rule four is that it utterly destroys provenance.

06:01.360 --> 06:02.340
Destroying provenance.

06:02.580 --> 06:05.020
Meaning, like, the history of the system is just gone.

06:05.320 --> 06:06.820
It burns the bridge it just crossed.

06:07.000 --> 06:08.360
It doesn't just burn the bridge.

06:08.500 --> 06:11.000
It vaporizes the bridge, scatters the ashes,

06:11.000 --> 06:12.680
and then paves over the river.

06:13.180 --> 06:15.860
Once you are on the stable manifold M,

06:16.280 --> 06:18.640
there is no efficient procedure in the universe

06:18.640 --> 06:23.860
that can tell you exactly which microscopic path in X you took to get there.

06:24.080 --> 06:24.400
Wow.

06:24.400 --> 06:28.660
And the central thesis of Fliction's paper is that this destruction isn't a glitch.

06:29.000 --> 06:31.020
It isn't a failure of our measuring tools.

06:31.460 --> 06:36.560
The functional output is actually more stable because the path to it was systematically destroyed.

06:36.820 --> 06:41.080
Let me try out an analogy here because my brain is really trying to make this concrete for you all listening.

06:41.080 --> 06:44.100
It sounds a bit like baking a cake.

06:44.520 --> 06:44.640
Okay.

06:44.800 --> 06:45.280
I like that.

06:45.520 --> 06:46.040
Think about it.

06:46.080 --> 06:48.660
You have this chaotic, microscopic space,

06:49.240 --> 06:51.380
X the flour particles flying through the air,

06:51.480 --> 06:52.300
the milk splashing,

06:52.480 --> 06:55.900
the highly specific trajectory of your whisk moving through the batter,

06:56.260 --> 06:59.400
the chaotic thermodynamic heat vectors inside the oven.

06:59.480 --> 06:59.760
Right.

06:59.940 --> 07:00.540
Highly complex.

07:00.580 --> 07:02.000
That's all incredibly complex.

07:02.000 --> 07:04.220
But once that cake is baked,

07:04.520 --> 07:06.140
once you have your functional invariant,

07:06.580 --> 07:11.460
you absolutely cannot reverse engineer the exact trajectory of the whisk.

07:11.600 --> 07:13.480
You can't look at a slice of cake and say,

07:13.820 --> 07:17.940
ah, yes, the baker stirred clockwise at exactly three miles per hour,

07:18.060 --> 07:21.040
and the 400th grain of flour landed in the bottom left corner.

07:21.260 --> 07:21.700
Exactly.

07:21.960 --> 07:23.960
The provenance is completely destroyed.

07:24.540 --> 07:26.060
But the cake is stable.

07:26.260 --> 07:27.860
That is a fantastic analogy,

07:27.860 --> 07:31.480
but I want to push it even further to really capture Fliction's point.

07:31.480 --> 07:32.800
Because in your analogy,

07:33.400 --> 07:36.960
the destruction of the whisk's path might just seem like a side effect of the heat.

07:37.340 --> 07:40.200
What this paper is arguing is that in admissible systems,

07:40.620 --> 07:45.040
the destruction of the whisk's path is the absolute requirement for the cake to exist at all.

07:45.080 --> 07:46.960
Wait, you're saying the chaos is actually necessary?

07:47.200 --> 07:48.360
Completely necessary.

07:48.780 --> 07:50.800
Entropy, that thermodynamic chaos in the oven,

07:51.100 --> 07:55.160
acts as a flow that actively consumes the trajectory of the individual ingredients

07:55.160 --> 07:57.380
so that the stable invariant can persist.

07:57.380 --> 08:02.320
Imagine if you tried to perfectly preserve the exact microscopic location and trajectory

08:02.320 --> 08:05.580
of every single grain of flour and every drop of egg.

08:05.660 --> 08:07.020
I mean, you'd just have a mess.

08:07.360 --> 08:09.120
You would never get a unified cake.

08:09.520 --> 08:14.180
You would just have this rigidly suspended, highly fragile matrix of raw ingredients.

08:14.760 --> 08:17.740
The moment you jostled the pan, the whole structure would fail.

08:17.920 --> 08:19.160
Oh, that makes so much sense.

08:19.160 --> 08:23.940
Right. By letting the microscopic paths collapse into a blended entropic mess,

08:24.300 --> 08:28.840
the macroscopic object, the cake becomes highly resilient to noise.

08:29.240 --> 08:32.820
You can slice it, drop it, freeze it, and it's still a cake.

08:33.180 --> 08:35.680
Okay, so the cake is the admissible projection.

08:36.100 --> 08:38.700
The path is destroyed, but the function survives.

08:38.980 --> 08:39.160
Yeah.

08:39.160 --> 08:42.740
So to see this mathematical cake baking in actual practice,

08:42.960 --> 08:45.880
the paper takes us to our first domain, which is cryptography.

08:46.700 --> 08:50.320
Specifically, a subfield called secure multi-party computation.

08:50.820 --> 08:52.740
And the underlying question here is,

08:53.100 --> 08:56.340
how do a group of people compute a function together

08:56.340 --> 08:59.040
without ever sharing their private inputs with each other?

08:59.100 --> 09:02.480
Right, which is historically a massive problem in computer science.

09:02.800 --> 09:07.180
Let's say 10 hospitals want to calculate the average age of their patients to track a disease,

09:07.180 --> 09:12.140
but federal privacy laws make it totally illegal for them to share their individual patient databases.

09:12.840 --> 09:15.740
Historically, you would solve this with a trusted third party.

09:16.460 --> 09:20.780
The hospitals build a secure pipeline, send their data to a central supercomputer,

09:21.400 --> 09:23.960
the computer does the math, and sends the answer back.

09:24.060 --> 09:25.340
That's the classic pipeline.

09:25.480 --> 09:25.700
Yeah.

09:25.820 --> 09:26.900
But pipelines can be hacked.

09:27.200 --> 09:28.960
I mean, the central server can be compromised.

09:29.220 --> 09:29.700
Exactly.

09:29.940 --> 09:35.500
So Flexchain points to a completely different framework called Additive Randomized Encodings, or RE.

09:35.500 --> 09:41.600
The paper references foundational work by Halevi from 2023 and Batansky from 2025.

09:42.540 --> 09:44.520
The ARE framework asks,

09:45.120 --> 09:47.240
what if we don't use secure pipelines at all?

09:47.560 --> 09:51.340
What if the only global operation allowed on the network is pure addition?

09:51.900 --> 09:55.840
No authenticated encrypted channels, no back and forth secret messaging rounds,

09:56.060 --> 09:57.700
just throwing numbers into a pile.

09:57.860 --> 10:01.980
But if you just throw your private data into a public pile, everyone can see it.

10:02.040 --> 10:02.800
How is that secure?

10:02.800 --> 10:05.500
This is where Batansky's 2025 paper comes in.

10:05.740 --> 10:09.240
They proved mathematically that you can compute literally any function securely

10:09.240 --> 10:11.980
just by adding random cryptographic garbage together.

10:12.360 --> 10:15.320
Let's break down the ORR function gadget they use in the paper,

10:15.440 --> 10:17.940
because it perfectly illustrates the collapse of provenance.

10:18.020 --> 10:19.720
Okay, an OR function, just for the listener.

10:19.940 --> 10:23.160
An ORR function just asks, is at least one of these things true?

10:23.400 --> 10:23.640
Right.

10:23.640 --> 10:25.820
So let's say we have a network of computers,

10:25.820 --> 10:30.340
and we want to know if at least one computer has detected a specific malware virus.

10:30.660 --> 10:34.120
We don't want to know which computer, because that reveals network vulnerabilities.

10:34.500 --> 10:36.960
We just want a yes or no for the whole network.

10:37.640 --> 10:42.840
In the RA framework, if a computer's input is zero, meaning it has no virus,

10:43.080 --> 10:47.000
it encodes its answer as the mathematical identity element.

10:47.000 --> 10:49.220
Which, in plain math, is just a zero.

10:49.420 --> 10:51.200
Basically, yes, it contributes a zero.

10:51.720 --> 10:54.700
But if the input is one, meaning it does have the virus,

10:54.960 --> 10:56.580
the computer does not submit a one.

10:57.020 --> 11:00.560
Instead, it encodes its answer as a completely random group element.

11:00.960 --> 11:05.600
It generates a massive, uniformly distributed string of pure cryptographic noise,

11:05.960 --> 11:06.760
and submits that.

11:06.980 --> 11:10.340
So everyone just throws their encoded answers into a giant anonymous shuffle.

11:10.440 --> 11:10.900
They do.

11:11.240 --> 11:14.180
And the evaluator on the other end, the person trying to find the answer,

11:14.180 --> 11:16.020
just adds everything in the shuffle together.

11:16.360 --> 11:18.440
Now, think about the two possible outcomes.

11:18.720 --> 11:21.440
If nobody has the virus, everyone threw in a zero.

11:21.740 --> 11:24.140
Zero plus zero plus zero equals zero.

11:24.340 --> 11:27.120
The evaluator looks at the sum, sees a zero,

11:27.460 --> 11:30.380
and knows the answer to the OR function is false.

11:30.740 --> 11:34.140
But the moment even one single computer has a flag raised,

11:34.660 --> 11:36.660
they throw in that massive random number.

11:36.820 --> 11:38.880
And here's the absolute genius of the system.

11:38.980 --> 11:43.860
The moment you add one truly random, uniformly distributed element

11:43.860 --> 11:46.780
to a sum of zeros, what happens to the sum?

11:47.040 --> 11:48.460
It just becomes that random number.

11:48.460 --> 11:52.920
Yes, but more fundamentally, the entire aggregate state of the system

11:52.920 --> 11:55.360
undergoes a sudden phase transition.

11:55.940 --> 11:57.700
The sum becomes completely thermalized.

11:57.840 --> 12:00.680
It instantly shifts from being highly structured all zeros

12:00.680 --> 12:04.060
to pure, unadulterated randomness.

12:04.200 --> 12:06.980
So the evaluator just sees a giant string of random nonsense.

12:07.200 --> 12:07.560
Exactly.

12:07.820 --> 12:09.760
The evaluator looks at the final sum and says,

12:09.820 --> 12:12.480
well, it's a random string of nonsense, so it is definitely not zero.

12:12.480 --> 12:15.440
Therefore, the answer to the OR function is yes,

12:15.700 --> 12:17.540
at least one computer has the virus.

12:17.900 --> 12:19.920
But because the sum is completely random,

12:20.360 --> 12:22.680
the evaluator has absolutely no way of knowing

12:22.680 --> 12:24.760
which computers submitted the random number.

12:25.420 --> 12:27.820
Furthermore, if three computers had the virus,

12:28.180 --> 12:30.160
they would have submitted three different random numbers.

12:30.740 --> 12:34.160
The sum of three random numbers is just another random number.

12:34.280 --> 12:34.820
Oh, wow.

12:34.960 --> 12:36.220
So looking at the final output,

12:36.320 --> 12:39.760
you can't tell if one person triggered it or 10 people triggered it.

12:39.760 --> 12:42.680
You have literally no idea whose input was what.

12:42.980 --> 12:45.180
This is the literal destruction of provenance.

12:45.620 --> 12:49.220
The function, the yes or no answer, survived perfectly.

12:49.780 --> 12:54.200
But the microscopic path, who submitted what and how many submitted it,

12:54.320 --> 12:56.320
has completely collapsed into noise.

12:56.440 --> 12:59.320
That makes total sense for a simple yes, no, or R function.

12:59.740 --> 13:01.320
But Fliction's paper goes deeper.

13:01.740 --> 13:04.720
It talks about how they lift this simple trick to perform complex,

13:04.720 --> 13:08.420
universal computation using something called a leaky AR.

13:08.800 --> 13:10.000
And if I'm following the logic,

13:10.080 --> 13:11.760
it's basically a system that mostly works,

13:11.760 --> 13:14.080
but it has a flaw where it accidentally leaks

13:14.080 --> 13:16.920
a tiny bit of your private input when the output is a one.

13:17.180 --> 13:18.040
That's a really good summary.

13:18.860 --> 13:19.980
To do complex math,

13:20.120 --> 13:22.420
the random noise generation isn't quite perfect.

13:23.100 --> 13:25.360
There is a microscopic statistical correlation

13:25.360 --> 13:27.900
that a supercomputer could potentially exploit

13:27.900 --> 13:29.140
to guess an input.

13:29.540 --> 13:30.080
It leaks.

13:30.460 --> 13:32.060
And obviously, in cryptography,

13:32.280 --> 13:34.620
a tiny leak is a catastrophic failure.

13:35.680 --> 13:37.740
So how do they fix it without building a pipeline?

13:38.560 --> 13:41.520
I read something in the paper about secret sharing and error bounds,

13:41.660 --> 13:43.380
but the math went right over my head.

13:43.440 --> 13:45.940
Let's demystify it because it's actually quite elegant.

13:46.320 --> 13:49.920
They use an amplification technique via additive secret sharing.

13:50.420 --> 13:53.020
Let's say you need a security parameter of tau.

13:53.420 --> 13:56.140
Think of tau as your required level of paranoia.

13:56.140 --> 13:57.220
Say, 100.

13:57.760 --> 13:59.460
Instead of submitting your input once,

13:59.760 --> 14:01.620
you mathematically split your single input

14:01.620 --> 14:04.020
into 100 different random-looking puzzle pieces,

14:04.020 --> 14:05.880
and these are the secret shares.

14:06.060 --> 14:09.200
Like breaking a $10 bill into a 5, 4, 1s, and 4 quarters,

14:09.660 --> 14:11.100
but with cryptographic numbers.

14:11.420 --> 14:11.800
Close.

14:11.980 --> 14:13.120
But with secret sharing,

14:13.440 --> 14:17.420
any single puzzle piece by itself looks like pure random noise.

14:17.960 --> 14:20.380
You absolutely must have all 100 pieces

14:20.380 --> 14:21.920
to reconstruct the original input.

14:22.300 --> 14:23.820
If you have 99 pieces,

14:23.820 --> 14:26.520
you still know literally nothing about the original input.

14:26.520 --> 14:28.720
So you take your 100 secret shares,

14:28.920 --> 14:33.220
and you run that leaky, imperfect protocol 100 parallel times.

14:33.360 --> 14:34.740
Okay, but if the protocol is leaky,

14:34.840 --> 14:37.020
